Z||000000||GT Bastard||08-19-2000||12:01 AM||||Just curious if anyone has had trouble with slave cylinders going bad...  A friend of mine has gone through several in his '86SE 4-sp over the course of 3 years.  I just replaced one myself a couple weeks ago and it's been fine ever since.  I've heard of others complaining of the same problem.  All I can think of is heating issues.... He's tried using AC Delco, Wagner, and a couple others...  My slave runs dangerously close the crossover pipe(aftermarket headers) so I wrapped the slave in header-wrap until I fabricate a heat shield of some kind.   ||||reg||1
Z||000001||2birds||08-19-2000||12:07 AM||||Mine has never leaked visibly, but the clutch pedal engagement height gets farther down if I do a long highway trip.<BR>I'm replacing mine because it's the only way I can get a new bleeder screw.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Terrible Tom||08-19-2000||01:07 AM||||122K on my 86SE 4 speed with no problems....Of course, now that I say that, it will probably crap out next week.<p>[This message has been edited by Terrible Tom (edited 08-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||08-19-2000||09:49 AM||||when i put my car back together, last spring, i replaced the slave with a new GM one.  i also, did not use DOT 3... i used an pricey substitute... cant remember the name at the moment..<P>sorry tio digress... my point is, you need to use a quality slave cylinder, and avoid these cheap DOT 3/4 products... use something like , Super Blue or Red Line brake fluid (Super Blue being the recomended one), that is a product used in race cars and <B>will not</B> get weak due to the heat of the engine!<P>also, if you can find a heat shield for the slave cylinder, i highly recomend it.  i got one from Josh (TheFiero64) and am going to make a sketch of it to send to Rodney Dickman. (i looked for a couple of months for one of these)<P>anyway, hope this helps.<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pontiaddict||08-19-2000||03:12 PM||||Hoop was it DOT 5 silicone based fluid?<BR>DOT 4 used to be synthetic racing fluid. It was good in high heat conditions, but needs to be changed alot due to it absorbing water really easy. I think it is used in new cars now and i'm not sure if it's the same thing.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phoenix||08-20-2000||10:25 AM||||A good fluid to use in both the clutch and brakes is Castrol LMA.  It has a high boiling point and has a low moisture absorbtion (LMA). I have used it for years for high speed auto-x's - never "boiled" the brakes once.||||reg||1
